<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/maui/design"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:viewModels="clr-namespace:Plutus.Frontend.AppClient.ViewModels.MainTill.Inventory.Items"
             xmlns:i18n="clr-namespace:I18N_L10N.Extensions;assembly=I18N_L10N"
             mc:Ignorable="d"
             x:Class="Plutus.Frontend.AppClient.Views.MainTill.Inventory.Items.ViewAllView"
             Title="{Binding Title}">
    <ContentPage.BindingContext>
        <viewModels:ViewAllViewModel/>
    </ContentPage.BindingContext>
    <!--
        ⚠ A GRID, NOT A STACKLAYOUT, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE FIX FOR THE SHORT LIST.

        This was a `StackLayout`, which in MAUI measures each child to its DESIRED height and does
        not distribute what is left over. `VerticalOptions="FillAndExpand"` did that in
        Xamarin.Forms and is effectively a NO-OP in MAUI — one of the quieter differences between
        the two, because nothing warns and the screen still renders. The list asked for a modest
        height, got exactly that, and scrolled 20,000 items inside a ~230px box with the rest of the
        window empty below it.

        `RowDefinition Height="*"` is what actually gives the list the remaining space.

        ⚠ NO SYNCFUSION ON THIS SCREEN ANY MORE. Matt, 2026-08-10: *"I am not going to renew
        Syncfusion, it seems like it can be replaced."* It was an `SfListView` with an `SfPopup`
        context menu; it is now a plain MAUI `CollectionView`. What that swap costs and what it
        buys:
          • grouping moved into the VIEWMODEL (`ItemGroups`) — no shared mutable DataSource between
            view and viewmodel, which is what the crash-on-open bug was;
          • the header is a fixed Grid ABOVE the list rather than a scrolling HeaderTemplate, so the
            column titles no longer scroll away;
          • the right-click/long-press menu is gone — replaced by the visible per-row Edit button
            and tap-to-choose, which is what Matt asked for anyway ("Having an 'Edit item' button
            would be better than assuming somebody knew to tap on a row").
        ⚠ MAUI bindings fail SILENTLY, so this file is the one most likely to regress without a
        symptom. Every binding here resolves against `ItemModel` except the two marked otherwise.
    -->
    <ContentPage.Content>
        <Grid RowSpacing="0">
            <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
            </Grid.RowDefinitions>

            <Grid Grid.Row="0" ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
                <SearchBar Grid.Column="0"
                           Placeholder="{Binding Source={i18n:Translate Items}, StringFormat={i18n:Translate SearchArg}}"
                           Text="{Binding SearchText}"/>
                <!-- ⚠ A VISIBLE "Add item", for the same reason Edit got one: a capability reachable
                     only by a gesture nobody advertises is a capability that does not exist. The
                     till's unknown-barcode flow reaches the SAME command with the scanned code
                     already filled in. -->
                <Button Grid.Column="1" Text="Add item" Margin="4,0"
                        Command="{Binding CreateItemCommand}"/>
            </Grid>

            <!-- ⚠ FOUR columns, and the row template below MUST match. A header that disagrees with
                 its rows misaligns every price on the screen. It sits OUTSIDE the list on purpose:
                 as a CollectionView header it would scroll away with the first group. -->
            <Grid Grid.Row="1" Padding="6,4">
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width=".7*"/>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width=".1*"/>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width=".1*"/>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="90"/>
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <Label Grid.Column="0" FontSize="Medium" FontAttributes="Bold" Text="{i18n:Translate Name}"/>
                <Label Grid.Column="1" FontSize="Medium" FontAttributes="Bold" Text="{i18n:Translate Price}"/>
                <Label Grid.Column="2" FontSize="Medium" FontAttributes="Bold" Text="{i18n:Translate Stock}"/>
            </Grid>

            <!-- ⚠ An honest empty state. A blank list with no message reads as "this shop sells
                 nothing" — which is precisely how the legacy-table bug (the list read a table that
                 is empty for ever on a portal till) stayed hidden for as long as it did. -->
            <Label Grid.Row="2" Text="{Binding EmptyNotice}" IsVisible="{Binding ShowEmptyNotice}"
                   HorizontalOptions="Center" VerticalOptions="Center" Margin="20"
                   HorizontalTextAlignment="Center" TextColor="Gray"/>

            <CollectionView x:Name="ItemsListView" Grid.Row="2"
                            ItemsSource="{Binding ItemGroups}"
                            IsGrouped="True"
                            SelectionMode="Single"
                            SelectionChanged="OnRowSelected">
                <CollectionView.GroupHeaderTemplate>
                    <DataTemplate>
                        <!-- ⚠ Binds to `ItemGroup.Key`, NOT to ItemModel. -->
                        <Label Text="{Binding Key}" FontAttributes="Bold" FontSize="Medium"
                               Padding="6,8,6,4" BackgroundColor="{AppThemeBinding Light=#EEEEEE, Dark=#303030}"/>
                    </DataTemplate>
                </CollectionView.GroupHeaderTemplate>
                <CollectionView.ItemTemplate>
                    <DataTemplate>
                        <Frame CornerRadius="2" Padding="1" Margin="1">
                            <Frame.HasShadow>
                                <OnPlatform x:TypeArguments="x:Boolean">
                                    <On Platform="Android, Windows">True</On>
                                    <On Platform="iOS">False</On>
                                </OnPlatform>
                            </Frame.HasShadow>
                            <Grid>
                                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                    <ColumnDefinition Width=".7*"/>
                                    <ColumnDefinition Width=".1*"/>
                                    <ColumnDefinition Width=".1*"/>
                                    <ColumnDefinition Width="90"/>
                                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                <Label Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding Name}" VerticalOptions="Center"/>
                                <Label Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding Price, StringFormat='{0:C}'}" VerticalOptions="Center"/>
                                <!-- ⚠ `StockDisplay`, NOT `Stock.Quantity`. `Stock` is a legacy EF
                                     navigation property that nothing populates on a
                                     portal-provisioned till, and a MAUI binding to a null path
                                     renders BLANK without complaint — so this column was empty on
                                     every row, and an empty stock column reads as ZERO. -->
                                <Label Grid.Column="2" Text="{Binding StockDisplay}" VerticalOptions="Center"/>

                                <!-- ⚠ A VISIBLE EDIT BUTTON, on every row. Matt, 2026-08-10:
                                     "Having an 'Edit item' button would be better than assuming
                                     somebody knew to tap on a row." He is right — editing lived
                                     only on a right-click menu, then only on a tap, and neither
                                     says so on screen. A control you have to be told about is one
                                     nobody uses.

                                     ⚠ The command lives on the VIEWMODEL, not on the row, so it is
                                     reached through the list by name — `Source={x:Reference
                                     ItemsListView}`. A plain `{Binding …}` here would resolve
                                     against `ItemModel`, find nothing, and render a button that
                                     silently does nothing when pressed. -->
                                <Button Grid.Column="3" Text="{i18n:Translate Edit}"
                                        FontSize="Micro" Padding="0" HeightRequest="32" Margin="4,0"
                                        Command="{Binding Path=BindingContext.OpenEditItemCommandArg, Source={x:Reference ItemsListView}}"
                                        CommandParameter="{Binding Id}"/>
                            </Grid>
                        </Frame>
                    </DataTemplate>
                </CollectionView.ItemTemplate>
            </CollectionView>

            <!-- ⚠ A TRUNCATED LIST MUST SAY SO. The browse read is capped at 500 rows against a
                 catalogue that can hold twenty thousand, and a cap with no marker reads as
                 completeness — an operator scrolling to "R" concludes the rest was deleted. -->
            <Label Grid.Row="3" Text="{Binding CapNotice}" IsVisible="{Binding ShowCapNotice}"
                   FontSize="Micro" TextColor="Gray" Padding="6,4"
                   HorizontalTextAlignment="Center"/>
        </Grid>
    </ContentPage.Content>
</ContentPage>
